 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 550748,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/64e17bb6bf0697c8.jpg\" alt=\"A food web with four layers labeled A at the top to D at the bottom is illustrated. The bottom layer is diatoms. These are eaten by the next layer, which has zebra or quagga mussels and chironomids. These are eaten by the second from the top layer, which is lake whitefish, round goby and slimy sculpin. The top layer has sea lamprey, burbot and lake trout. Sea lamprey eats burbot, lake trout, lake whitefish, round goby and slimy sculpin.\"></p>\n<p>This figure shows a food web of an ecosystem. What would happen to the food web if all of the species of the entire row B was wiped out by a natural disaster such as an oil spill? Why?</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1082620,
     "content_html": "The producers may die out, causing the food web to collapse."
    },
    {
     "id": 1082621,
     "content_html": "The food web would not be affected as the secondary and tertiary consumers would become primary and secondary consumers, respectively."
    },
    {
     "id": 1082622,
     "content_html": "The tertiary consumers would die out due to lack of food and this could potentially lead to collapse of the entire food web."
    },
    {
     "id": 1082623,
     "content_html": "The food web might suffer from loss of primary producers for a while which would then recover quickly, thus, reviving the ecosystem."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
